.PP
# d3\&.\fBtimeTickInterval\fP(\fIstart\fP, \fIstop\fP, \fIcount\fP) · \fRSource\fP

.PP
Returns the time interval that would be used by \fBd3\&.timeTicks\fP given the same arguments\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fButcTicks\fP(\fIstart\fP, \fIstop\fP, \fIcount\fP) · \fRSource\fP

.PP
Returns an array of approximately \fIcount\fP dates at regular intervals between \fIstart\fP and \fIstop\fP (inclusive)\&. If \fIstop\fP is before \fIstart\fP, dates are returned in reverse chronological order; otherwise dates are returned in chronological order\&. The following UTC time intervals are considered:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 second
.IP "\(bu" 2
5 seconds
.IP "\(bu" 2
15 seconds
.IP "\(bu" 2
30 seconds
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 minute
.IP "\(bu" 2
5 minutes
.IP "\(bu" 2
15 minutes
.IP "\(bu" 2
30 minutes
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 hour
.IP "\(bu" 2
3 hours
.IP "\(bu" 2
6 hours
.IP "\(bu" 2
12 hours
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 day
.IP "\(bu" 2
2 days
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 week
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 month
.IP "\(bu" 2
3 months
.IP "\(bu" 2
1 year
.PP

.PP
Multiples of milliseconds (for small ranges) and years (for large ranges) are also considered, following the rules of \fRd3\&.ticks\fP\&. The interval producing the number of dates that is closest to \fIcount\fP is used\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
start = new Date(Date\&.UTC(1970, 2, 1))
stop = new Date(Date\&.UTC(1996, 2, 19))
count = 4
d3\&.utcTicks(start, stop, count) // [1975\-01\-01, 1980\-01\-01, 1985\-01\-01, 1990\-01\-01, 1995\-01\-01]
.fi
.PP

.PP
If \fIcount\fP is a time interval, this function behaves similarly to \fB*interval*\&.range\fP except that both \fIstart\fP and \fIstop\fP are inclusive and it may return dates in reverse chronological order if \fIstop\fP is before \fIstart\fP\&.

.PP
# d3\&.\fButcTickInterval\fP(\fIstart\fP, \fIstop\fP, \fIcount\fP) · \fRSource\fP

.PP
Returns the time interval that would be used by \fBd3\&.utcTicks\fP given the same arguments\&. If there is no associated interval, such as when \fIstart\fP or \fIstop\fP is invalid, returns null\&. 
